\chapter{Allocation, containers, and storage kernels}
\label{ch:101}
\index{Allocation, containers, and storage kernels}

Allocation and container kernels provide reusable C storage policies. An arena groups allocations under an arena lifetime. A vector maintains logical length and capacity. A bitset maps logical positions into word storage, and serialization or traversal needs the caller's agreed bit numbering and bounds. Each handle or pointer borrows or owns memory according to its concrete API. Growth can invalidate saved pointers into a buffer.

\section{Test the allocation contract}
Fill a container to capacity and grow it with a controlled allocator. Check the contents through the container's current accessors, and inspect the manual before reusing any old address. Exercise allocation failure and verify the documented return status and resulting state. If several kernel objects use a caller allocator, associate ownership with the allocator domain that must release them. For On1x integration, prefer a documented bridge or wrap a bounded copy-safe operation. Passing the address of an internal C buffer into a raw integer signature loses the lifetime and representation checks.

\section{Push an element already inside a vector}
Start with an integer vector containing the values zero through seven.
In the current implementation the first successful push into fresh
storage reserves room for eight elements, so the ninth push needs more
space. Take the address returned by \OnxCodeTerm{mulib\_vec\_at} for
index two and pass it directly to push. Predict the contents if growth
succeeds: the first eight integers remain in their original order and
the ninth integer is two. This case is more demanding than pushing a
stack local, because growing storage may move the very bytes that the
operation is supposed to copy.

Read the implementation in temporal order. Before reserve, push detects
that its source lies in the vector and saves its offset. It checks that
an entire element starting there is within the live bytes, rather than
merely inside some spare capacity. After a successful reserve it forms
the source address from the new base plus the saved offset. Only after
the byte copy does it increase length. The caller does not inherit this
rebasing service: look up index two again before reading it.

Exercise the failure branch without depending on a particular machine
running out of RAM. With stride greater than one, request a reserve of
\OnxCodeTerm{SIZE\_MAX} elements. The byte count cannot fit, so reserve
returns zero and length, capacity and contents should remain as before.
Finally clear the vector. Capacity still exists, but index zero now
returns null because indexing is against logical length. Free releases
the storage; neither clear nor free runs a destructor inside a stored
element.

For example, storing a C pointer in each slot copies the pointer
value; clearing ten such slots would not free the ten pointed-to
objects. Arrange their cleanup while the entries are still accessible.

